    },
    "message": "docs(optimizer): the trimming module's prefix-stability claim was false\n\nThe module said:\n\n    The transform is deterministic. The same result trims to the same bytes\n    on every turn, so a conversation prefix stays stable and Anthropic prompt\n    caching keeps hitting after the one turn on which a result first crosses\n    the threshold.\n\nPer-request determinism is not cross-request prefix stability. A result\nchanges on TWO occasions: when it first crosses the threshold, and again when\nit ages out of protect_recent_results. The second rewrites bytes in the middle\nof an already-established prefix, which is the expensive one, and the\ndocstring did not mention it.\n\nMeasured end to end -- real Settings, real MessagesHandler trim path, real\nOpenAI-chat provider, over loopback to a stub modelling prefix caching, 24\nturns. Positive control reproduced 91.8-94.9% cache hit (the shape of real\ntraffic); negative control with a mutated prefix collapsed to 0.0%. Same sign\nat cache-block granularity 1, 128 and 1024, so the conclusion is not an\nartifact of the cache model.\n\n    protect_recent        fresh tokens   cache hit   chars removed\n    0                           72,897       91.9%      15,024,408\n    1                          486,278       62.5%      13,781,644\n    2 (shipped default)        521,860       69.1%      12,561,418\n    4                          955,949       59.8%      10,409,077\n    trimming off               470,648       91.8%               0\n\nSo the shipped default costs 10.9% MORE fresh input tokens than not trimming\nat all, and enabling it mid-conversation costs one near-total cache miss --\n3.8% hit and 107,797 fresh tokens on that single turn. Break-even is a\nbaseline hit rate around 90.9%: below it trimming wins, above it it loses. On\na log where five providers sit at 84-98%, that is the losing side.\n\nThe cost is entirely the recency guard, not trimming. At protect_recent=0 the\nper-turn prefix match is 100.00% on every turn, the hit rate is unchanged\n(91.9% vs 91.8%), fresh tokens fall 84.5%, and it removes MORE content than\nthe default -- because a result is then trimmed the moment it appears and\nnever mutates again.\n\nThe default was deliberately NOT changed. protect_recent=0 means the model\nnever sees the full text of the read it just issued, and that answer-quality\ncost is unmeasured. A measured cache win does not justify an unmeasured\nquality loss, and the feature ships off regardless.\n\nDocs only: the docstring and the .env.example block now carry the table and\nthe break-even figure, so nobody enables this without meeting the number\nfirst. No behaviour change, no default change, no new setting.\n\nVerification\n- 765 passed across the trimming and config suites.\n- ruff format, ruff check, ty, suppression grep: clean.\n\nCo-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>\nClaude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01Y6SPhyApg7trDx44SJqNti",
